Pope Francis is in Ireland this morning  for the first visit by a pope in almost  four decades to one of Europe's most  Catholic countries the visit comes amid  global condemnation of the church's  handling of sex crimes rocking the  church  Jonathan Vigliotti is in Dublin good  morning Jonathan good morning and the  Pope speaking for the first time inside  the Dublin Castle behind me where he  took a brief moment in that speech to  address child abuse in churches  specifically here in Ireland he called  them crimes but most noticeably absent  from that speech any reference to the  abuse allegations in Pennsylvania the  Pope's weekend visit to celebrate  Catholic faith and compassion has been  overshadowed by the explosive grand jury  report in Pennsylvania cataloguing abuse  by more than 300 priests the last Pope  to visit Ireland was John Paul the  second in 1979 who was greeted then by  two and a half million faithful five  times the crowd expected for Pope  Francis and I believed him I don't  really meant something to me and a year  and a half later I was raped by a priest  for the first time and that continued  for two and a half years Tom oar Gorman  says he was abused when he was 14 years  old and they covered up those crimes and  they allowed them to go unchecked a  series of abuse allegations have taken  their toll in Ireland one of the most  Catholic countries in the world church  attendance has plummeted in May abortion  was legalized in what many called a  referendum against the church Irish nun  Liz Murphy who we sat down with earlier  this week said if the church is to  survive it will need total reform if  he's a very male masculine institutional  top-down dictatorial body who wouldn't  want as a woman to see that collapse and  Ireland's first gay prime minister also  had some tough words he spoke directly  to the Pope inside the castle behind me  telling the Pope that he must adhere to  a zero tolerance policy  against church abuse and also demanding  the Pope take action Anthony Jonathan  Vigliotti in Dublin for us this morning  Jonathan thanks so much
 

